<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-linear-inequalities-from-graphs" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Forming Linear Inequalities from Graphs</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.0 Numbers and Algebra
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        1.3 Linear Inequalities
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Form simple linear inequalities from inequality graphs
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Writing the linear inequalities that define a given shaded region.
         Suggested activity: Learners study a shaded graph and write down the inequalities that define the unshaded region.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Random shaded region generated by a button; learners type the inequalities and the applet checks them. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Essential 1.3 (e); outcome (f), appreciate the use of inequalities, placed here (Inequalities Around Us) with the Curriculum Links
         Activity: as planning notes (write the inequalities that define the unshaded region) | added: corners as a check
         JSXGraph: fig-inequality-from-graph (assets/jsxgraph/inequality-from-graph.js), as planning notes, with 12 preset graphs (not random) and a typed answer checked by the applet
         STACK: assets/stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/04-linear-inequalities/05-linear-inequalities-from-graphs/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> how to read a boundary's equation, its line style and the clear side.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> write one or several inequalities for a clear region, and check them with a test point in <m>R</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> read a rule back from a graph, such as the road rules for boda boda riders.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners work backwards from a drawn region to its inequalities.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Draw the activity figure on the board. Ask first: <q>Which side is wanted, the shaded or the clear?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-reading-a-region">
        <title>Reading a Region</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Work with a partner. Use <xref ref="fig-reading-a-clear-region"/>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-reading-a-clear-region">
                <caption>A region left clear by three boundaries.</caption>
                <image width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>x = 1 solid, y = 2 dashed, and a solid sloping line through (14, 0) and (0, 4); the unwanted sides shaded (left of x = 1, below y = 2, above the sloping line); R the clear triangle with corners (1, 2), (7, 2) and (1, 26/7). Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.45]
\draw[gray!40, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (15,6);
\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (-1,-1) -- (1,-1) -- (1,6) -- (-1,6) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (-1,-1) -- (15,-1) -- (15,2) -- (-1,2) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (15,-0.2857) -- (15,6) -- (-1,6) -- (-1,4.2857) -- cycle;
\draw[-stealth] (-1,0) -- (15+0.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-stealth] (0,-1) -- (0,6+0.6) node[above] {$y$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (2,0) {$2$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (4,0) {$4$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (6,0) {$6$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (8,0) {$8$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (10,0) {$10$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (12,0) {$12$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (14,0) {$14$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,2) {$2$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,4) {$4$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,6) {$6$};
\draw[very thick] (1,-1) -- (1,6);
\draw[very thick, dashed] (-1,2) -- (15,2);
\draw[very thick] (-1,4.2857) -- (15,-0.2857);
\node[font=\Large] at (3,2.8) {$R$};

\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (-1,-2.6) rectangle (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.6); \draw (-1,-2.6) rectangle (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.6);
\node[right, font=\small] at (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.3) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Write the equation of each boundary line. The sloping line passes through <m>(0, 4)</m> and <m>(14, 0)</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>x = 1</m>, <m>y = 2</m> and <m>2x + 7y = 28</m> (both <m>(0, 4)</m> and <m>(14, 0)</m> give <m>28</m>).</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Is each line solid or dashed?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>x = 1</m> solid, <m>y = 2</m> dashed, <m>2x + 7y = 28</m> solid.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Choose a point in <m>R</m>, for example <m>(2, 3)</m>. Which side of each line is it on?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>x = 2 \gt 1</m>; <m>y = 3 \gt 2</m>; <m>2(2) + 7(3) = 25 \lt 28</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Write the three inequalities that define <m>R</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>x \ge 1</m>, <m>y \gt 2</m>, <m>2x + 7y \le 28</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Check: find the corners of <m>R</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>(1, 2)</m>, <m>(7, 2)</m> and <m>(1, \tfrac{26}{7})</m>. Each satisfies the two equations of the lines that meet there.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Explain why the test point must come from the clear region.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The inequalities describe <m>R</m>, the clear region. A point from the shaded part would give the opposite sign for at least one line.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To fix the three steps for writing inequalities from a graph.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c) chose the direction of each sign from one point in <m>R</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Three steps: equation, line style, direction from a point in <m>R</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Vertical and horizontal lines give inequalities with one letter.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-inequalities-from-a-graph">
        <title>Inequalities from a Graph</title>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li><p>Find the equation of each boundary. A vertical line is <m>x = k</m> and a horizontal line is <m>y = h</m>. For a sloping line, use where it crosses the axes: a line through <m>(7, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 4)</m> is <m>4x + 7y = 28</m>, because both points give <m>28</m>. Or use its gradient and <m>y</m>-intercept: <m>y = mx + c</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>A solid line gives <m>\le</m> or <m>\ge</m>; a dashed line gives <m>\lt</m> or <m>\gt</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Choose the direction with a test point in the <em>clear</em> region <m>R</m>, not in the shaded part. Substitute it and choose the sign that makes the statement true.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-inequality-from-graph">
        <caption>Press New graph, type the inequality for the clear region and press Check.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-inequality-from-graph" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="5:6" source="jsxgraph/inequality-from-graph.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-inequality-from-graph" surface="jsxboard" aspect="5:6"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Press <alert>New graph</alert>. Find the equation of the boundary, decide whether it is solid or dashed, and pick a test point in the clear region <m>R</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Type the inequality for <m>R</m> in the box, for example <c>3x+7y&lt;=21</c>, and press <alert>Check</alert>. Act on the message.</p></li>
                        <li><p>When you are right, type the same inequality in a different form, such as <m>y \le \ldots</m>, and check it again.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-one-boundary">
        <title>One Boundary</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Write the inequality for the region <m>R</m> in <xref ref="fig-one-boundary"/>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-one-boundary">
                <caption>A region with one sloping boundary.</caption>
                <image width="60%">
                    <shortdescription>A solid line through (7, 0) and (0, 4); the side away from the origin shaded; R contains the origin. Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[gray!40, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (8,5);
\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (8,-0.5714) -- (8,5) -- (-1,5) -- (-1,4.5714) -- cycle;
\draw[-stealth] (-1,0) -- (8+0.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-stealth] (0,-1) -- (0,5+0.6) node[above] {$y$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (1,0) {$1$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (2,0) {$2$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (3,0) {$3$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (4,0) {$4$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (5,0) {$5$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (6,0) {$6$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (7,0) {$7$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (8,0) {$8$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,-1) {$-1$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,1) {$1$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,2) {$2$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,3) {$3$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,4) {$4$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,5) {$5$};
\draw[very thick] (-1,4.5714) -- (8,-0.5714);
\node[font=\Large] at (2,1.2) {$R$};

\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (-1,-2.6) rectangle (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.6); \draw (-1,-2.6) rectangle (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.6);
\node[right, font=\small] at (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.3) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The line passes through <m>(7, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 4)</m>, so it is <m>4x + 7y = 28</m>: check <m>4(7) = 28</m> and <m>7(4) = 28</m>.</p>
            <p>It is solid, so the sign is <m>\le</m> or <m>\ge</m>.</p>
            <p><m>R</m> contains <m>(0, 0)</m>: <m>4(0) + 7(0) = 0</m>, and <m>0 \le 28</m> is true. So <m>R</m> is <m>4x + 7y \le 28</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-a-clear-triangle">
        <title>A Clear Triangle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Write the three inequalities that define <m>R</m> in <xref ref="fig-clear-triangle"/>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-clear-triangle">
                <caption>A clear triangle.</caption>
                <image width="65%">
                    <shortdescription>x = 0 solid, y = 1 dashed, and a solid sloping line through (9, 0) and (0, 5); the unwanted sides shaded; R the clear triangle with corners (0, 1), (36/5, 1), (0, 5). Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.45]
\draw[gray!40, very thin] (-1,-1) grid (10,6);
\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (-1,-1) -- (0,-1) -- (0,6) -- (-1,6) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (-1,-1) -- (10,-1) -- (10,1) -- (-1,1) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (10,-0.5556) -- (10,6) -- (-1,6) -- (-1,5.5556) -- cycle;
\draw[-stealth] (-1,0) -- (10+0.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-stealth] (0,-1) -- (0,6+0.6) node[above] {$y$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (1,0) {$1$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (2,0) {$2$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (3,0) {$3$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (4,0) {$4$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (5,0) {$5$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (6,0) {$6$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (7,0) {$7$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (8,0) {$8$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (9,0) {$9$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (10,0) {$10$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,-1) {$-1$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,1) {$1$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,2) {$2$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,3) {$3$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,4) {$4$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,5) {$5$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,6) {$6$};
\draw[very thick] (0,-1) -- (0,6);
\draw[very thick, dashed] (-1,1) -- (10,1);
\draw[very thick] (-1,5.5556) -- (10,-0.5556);
\node[font=\Large] at (2.2,2.2) {$R$};

\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (-1,-2.6) rectangle (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.6); \draw (-1,-2.6) rectangle (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.6);
\node[right, font=\small] at (-1+0.8,-2.6+0.3) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The boundaries are <m>x = 0</m> (solid), <m>y = 1</m> (dashed) and the line through <m>(9, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 5)</m>, which is <m>5x + 9y = 45</m> (solid).</p>
            <p>Take <m>(1, 2)</m> in <m>R</m>: <m>1 \ge 0</m>; <m>2 \gt 1</m>; <m>5(1) + 9(2) = 23 \le 45</m>.</p>
            <p>So <m>R</m> is <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \gt 1</m>, <m>5x + 9y \le 45</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-using-the-gradient">
        <title>Using the Gradient</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A dashed line passes through <m>(0, -1)</m> and <m>(6, 4)</m>. The side above the line is shaded and <m>R</m> is below it, as in <xref ref="fig-gradient-boundary"/>. Write the inequality for <m>R</m>.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-gradient-boundary">
                <caption>A dashed boundary that crosses the <m>x</m>-axis between whole numbers.</caption>
                <image width="60%">
                    <shortdescription>A dashed line through (0, -1) and (6, 4); the side above it shaded; R below it. Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.5]
\draw[gray!40, very thin] (-2,-3) grid (8,6);
\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (8,5.6667) -- (8,6) -- (-2,6) -- (-2,-2.6667) -- cycle;
\draw[-stealth] (-2,0) -- (8+0.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-stealth] (0,-3) -- (0,6+0.6) node[above] {$y$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (-2,0) {$-2$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (1,0) {$1$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (2,0) {$2$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (3,0) {$3$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (4,0) {$4$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (5,0) {$5$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (6,0) {$6$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (7,0) {$7$};
\node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (8,0) {$8$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,-3) {$-3$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,-2) {$-2$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,-1) {$-1$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,1) {$1$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,2) {$2$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,3) {$3$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,4) {$4$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,5) {$5$};
\node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (0,6) {$6$};
\draw[very thick, dashed] (-2,-2.6667) -- (8,5.6667);
\node[font=\Large] at (5,0.5) {$R$};

\fill[gray!45, fill opacity=0.6] (-2,-4.6) rectangle (-2+0.8,-4.6+0.6); \draw (-2,-4.6) rectangle (-2+0.8,-4.6+0.6);
\node[right, font=\small] at (-2+0.8,-4.6+0.3) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>The gradient is <m>\dfrac{4 - (-1)}{6 - 0} = \dfrac{5}{6}</m> and the <m>y</m>-intercept is <m>-1</m>, so the line is <m>y = \tfrac{5}{6}x - 1</m>.</p>
            <p>It is dashed, so the sign is strict. <m>R</m> is below the line, and <m>(6, 0)</m> is in <m>R</m>: <m>0 \lt \tfrac{5}{6}(6) - 1 = 4</m>. So <m>R</m> is <m>y \lt \tfrac{5}{6}x - 1</m>.</p>
            <p>With whole-number coefficients: multiply by <m>6</m>, <m>6y \lt 5x - 6</m>, that is <m>5x - 6y \gt 6</m>. Check <m>(6, 0)</m>: <m>30 \gt 6</m>, true.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <paragraphs xml:id="paragraphs-inequalities-around-us">
        <title>Inequalities Around Us</title>
        <p>Inequalities describe the limits we live with every day.</p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Matatus are fitted with speed governors that keep them at or below <m>80\,\text{km/h}</m>. Lower speeds give drivers more time to stop, and save lives.</p></li>
                <li><p>Age limits: you must be at least <m>18</m> to get a driving licence or to vote.</p></li>
                <li><p>Safe loads: a boda boda may carry only one passenger, and a lorry must not exceed its greatest permitted load.</p></li>
                <li><p>A small business works within a budget and aims for a sales target: two inequalities in the numbers of items it buys and sells.</p></li>
                <li><p>Health workers keep vaccines between <m>2\,^{\circ}\text{C}</m> and <m>8\,^{\circ}\text{C}</m>, a compound inequality.</p></li>
                <li><p>Planning often means meeting several conditions at once. Drawing them as a region shows every choice that works, which is the starting point of linear programming.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>This answers the question we asked at the start of the chapter (<xref ref="kiq-linear-inequalities"/>): we use inequalities to write down rules and limits, to find every value or pair of values that obeys them, and to see those choices on a graph.</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Curriculum Links</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>How do we use inequalities in daily life? Revisit it with <xref ref="paragraphs-inequalities-around-us" text="title"/> and in the reflection.</p></li>
            <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Critical thinking, in the find-the-mistake questions of this chapter and in choosing a test point; communication and collaboration, in the activities' <q>explain to your partner</q> parts and in reading a graph aloud as inequalities.</p></li>
            <li><title>Values</title><p>Responsibility, in keeping to speed limits and safe loads, and in planning within a budget.</p></li>
            <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p>Road safety, in the speed-limit and load contexts (<xref ref="act-rules-on-the-road"/>, <xref ref="ex-nairobi-to-nakuru"/>, <xref ref="ex-boda-boda-rules"/>).</p></li>
            <li><title>Learning Outcome</title><p>This lesson also carries the outcome on appreciating the use of inequalities in real life.</p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type <m>\le</m> as <c>&lt;=</c> and <m>\ge</m> as <c>&gt;=</c>, for example <c>3x+10y&gt;=30</c>. Use the letters x and y as defined in the question. Type fractions, not decimals, for example <c>y&lt;=2x/3+1</c>.</p>

